        "type": "thinking",
        "thinking": "Since the style is whimsical rather than toon, I don't need kit docs here—I'll lean on soft rounded shapes, a cheerful palette, and paint techniques like grain, stripes, and countershading, plus decals or SVG work. I might peek at the samples folder briefly to see how similar files are structured before starting the design.\n\nFor the plane itself, I'm thinking a Stearman/Jenny-style biplane with a skeleton rooted at a central \"fuselage\" joint, branching into hinges for the propeller, wheels, ailerons, rudder, elevators, and steering. I want to add whimsical charm with a pilot figure wearing a scarf, goggles, and helmet, maybe a trailing banner reading \"SKY CIRCUS\" for fun. Scaling it realistically but exaggerated—around 7m long, 8.5m wingspan, 2.9m tall, with big chunky wheels and a round engine cowl—I'm now working out the coordinate placement: fuselage running along Z with the nose near z≈2.4 and tail near z≈-3.8, wheels set at roughly x=±0.95, z=0.9, axle height 0.5.\n\nI'm now planning the wing geometry—lower wing around y≈1.15 and upper wing at y≈2.4 with cabane and interplane struts connecting them, slight forward stagger, and rounded tips. Working through how to construct the wings as extruded or lofted airfoil shapes with proper thickness and bevel for rounded edges along the span.\n\nWriting wing cross-section logic...\n\nI'll trim the main wing chord so it ends before the trailing edge, then attach ailerons as separate thin extruded plates covering the back portion of the span, hinged along the wing's trailing edge line.\n\nEach aileron joint aims along the span axis (X), so rotating about the joint's own axis produces the twist motion needed for aileron deflection. I'll set up a root body joint for the fuselage that static parts attach to by default, while explicitly assigning bones to parts that need their own joints like the ailerons.\n\nFor the fuselage itself, I'm thinking a loft from nose to tail using catmull stations, with a rounded nose cowl, a wider cockpit section, and a gentle taper to a slim tail — giving it a chunky, whimsical silhouette. I want to check how the loft API handles cross-section shapes before committing to specific width/height values at each station.\n\nFor the engine, I'm imagining a classic radial-cowl look: a lathed barrel-shaped cowling in cheerful red, a cream or brass front ring, and a ring of small cylinder heads peeking out behind it for that Stearman-style detail. The propeller gets a brass spinner cone and two tapered blades mounted on a hinge joint aimed along the Z-axis so it can spin naturally about that bone.\n\nI'm also thinking through wooden two-blade prop tips painted with a whimsical yellow gradient, exhaust stacks, and pale-blue glass windscreens for the two cockpits. For the cockpits themselves, since carving an actual opening is tricky, I'll fake it with a dark inset \"tub\" shape ringed by a coaming sweep, placing the pilot in the rear seat and maybe a cute passenger—like a little dog—up front.\n\nI'm picturing the pilot with a round head, leather cap, big goggles, and a scarf swept back like it's flapping in the wind, while the dog has floppy ears, goggles, and a tongue sticking out. Static poses are fine for both.\n\nFor the tail, I'm building a vertical fin with a hinged rudder, horizontal stabilizers with hinged elevators, and a small spring-strutted tail wheel with its own steering hinge. The landing gear gets V-struts down to an axle with torus-shaped tires, hub disks, spokes, and cream sidewall decals, each wheel mounted on its own hinge joint aligned properly with the tire's rotation axis.\n\nNow I'm sizing the wheels low-poly and chunky for a whimsical look, adding cream hubcaps with red stripes, and setting the axle height so the wheel sinks slightly into the ground plane. I'm also laying out the wing bracing—N-pattern interplane struts, cabane struts to the upper wing, and thin rigging wires crossing diagonally between them—plus painting fabric rib stripes spaced evenly along the wingspan.\n\nI'm settling on a cheerful barnstormer color scheme: tomato red fuselage with a cream countershaded belly, sunshine yellow wings with red wingtip bands, cream rib lines, and maybe a star decal or scalloped sunburst detail on the cowl and tail for extra whimsy.\n\nTowing a banner seems impractical given its size, so instead I'll add a long streamer from the top wing tip and bunting draped between the interplane struts, with small triangular flags sweeping in a drooping curve.\n\nFor extra whimsy, I'm considering little touches: a small suitcase or trunk strapped to the turtledeck behind the cockpit, a lantern and oil can on the strut, a brass honk horn, a pitot tube, a headrest, a tiny rear-view mirror, and footholds for climbing in.\n\nShifting to the fuselage surface now, I'm figuring out how to procedurally paint panel lines, a cream cheat-line stripe with scallops along the sides, countershaded cream belly, and even rows of tiny rivet dots using position-based math on the loft coordinates.\n\n",
